(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacodynamics-exam-2-antimicrobials-quiz-1"]={"title":"Antimicrobials Quiz — Set 1","category":"Pharmacodynamics","questions":[{"q":"To which class does aztreonam belong?","choices":["Extended-spectrum penicillin","Carbapenem","Glycopeptide","Monobactam"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aztreonam is the monobactam beta-lactam. — Slide 31"},{"q":"Beta-lactam antibiotics kill bacteria by:","choices":["Binding irreversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit to block aminoacyl-tRNA attachment during translation","Inhibiting bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV to block DNA replication and transcription","Inactivating penicillin-binding proteins to block the last step of cell wall synthesis","Blocking peptidoglycan cross-linking as a glycopeptide rather than a beta-lactam"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams inactivate PBPs, the enzymes completing cell wall synthesis. — Slide 33"},{"q":"Vancomycin is:","choices":["Bacteriostatic","Only bacteriostatic when given orally","Bactericidal","Neither, it merely inhibits adhesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is bactericidal with time-dependent killing and moderate PAE. — Slide 44"},{"q":"Which agent is used for Rocky Mountain spotted fever and Lyme disease?","choices":["Azithromycin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline","Metronidazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline (a tetracycline) is indicated for RMSF and Lyme disease. — Slide 50"},{"q":"The disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol (flushing, nausea, vomiting, diaphoresis) is characteristic of:","choices":["Metronidazole","Doxycycline","Cephalexin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole causes a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol. — Slide 65"},{"q":"Which class binds the 30S ribosomal subunit?","choices":["Macrolides and clindamycin only","Beta-lactams and glycopeptides","Fluoroquinolones and rifampin","Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines bind the 30S subunit. — Slide 48"},{"q":"Vancomycin's antibacterial spectrum is:","choices":["Gram-negative only","Gram-positive only","Broad gram-positive and gram-negative","Anaerobes only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin covers gram-positives only. — Slide 44"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone is preferred for community-acquired pneumonia?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","Nalidixic acid","Levofloxacin","Trovafloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Levofloxacin, with improved gram-positive and Pseudomonas coverage, is used for CAP. — Slide 60-61"},{"q":"'Red man syndrome' (flushing from rapid infusion) is a distinctive adverse effect of:","choices":["Vancomycin","Amoxicillin","Doxycycline","Metronidazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid vancomycin infusion causes red man syndrome/flushing. — Slide 45"},{"q":"Natural penicillin (penicillin V) is:","choices":["Bacteriostatic, with essentially no PAE","Bactericidal with time-dependent killing and no PAE","Bactericidal with concentration-dependent killing","Bacteriostatic with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin V is bactericidal, time-dependent, with no PAE. — Slide 35"},{"q":"Which agent is a glycopeptide rather than a beta-lactam?","choices":["Vancomycin","Aztreonam","Cefepime","Piperacillin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is a glycopeptide, not a beta-lactam. — Slide 43"},{"q":"Metronidazole is:","choices":["Bactericidal","Bacteriostatic","Bacteriostatic against anaerobes only","Neither; it only inhibits toxin production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole is bactericidal with concentration-dependent killing and prolonged PAE. — Slide 63"},{"q":"Tetracyclines inhibit protein synthesis by:","choices":["Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit to block the translocation step of protein synthesis","Inhibiting bacterial DNA gyrase to prevent supercoiling during replication","Binding the 30S subunit and preventing tRNA from binding the mRNA-ribosome complex","Inactivating penicillin-binding proteins involved in cell wall synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines bind 30S and block tRNA attachment to the mRNA-ribosome complex. — Slide 49"},{"q":"Oral vancomycin has a specific indication for:","choices":["Systemic MRSA bacteremia and endocarditis","Uncomplicated gram-negative urinary tract infections","Outpatient community-acquired pneumonia","Severe C. difficile colitis (not orally absorbed)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral vancomycin is limited to severe C. difficile colitis because it acts locally in the gut. — Slide 44"},{"q":"Which class exerts concentration-dependent killing with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect?","choices":["Fluoroquinolones","Cephalosporins","Natural penicillins","Extended-spectrum penicillins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones show concentration-dependent killing with a prolonged PAE. — Slide 59"},{"q":"Metronidazole's spectrum centers on:","choices":["Aerobic gram-positive cocci like streptococci","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and other gram-negative rods","Anaerobes (gram-positive and gram-negative) and parasites","Atypical respiratory tract pathogens like Mycoplasma and Chlamydia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metronidazole covers gram-positive and gram-negative anaerobes plus parasites. — Slide 64"},{"q":"Clindamycin's coverage notably EXCLUDES:","choices":["Gram-positive aerobes","Gram-negative aerobes","Gram-negative anaerobes","Gram-positive anaerobes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin has NO gram-negative aerobic coverage. — Slide 55"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin generation offers maximal gram-negative activity without sacrificing gram-positive activity?","choices":["1st generation","2nd generation","3rd generation","4th generation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 4th generation (cefepime) has maximal gram-negative and gram-positive activity. — Slide 39-40"},{"q":"Adding clavulanic acid to amoxicillin (Augmentin) primarily:","choices":["Protects the beta-lactam and adds anaerobe (bacteroides) and MSSA coverage","Changes the drug's target from the cell wall to the bacterial ribosome","Converts the normally bactericidal drug into a bacteriostatic agent","Restricts its spectrum to gram-negative organisms exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The inhibitor protects the beta-lactam and adds anaerobe/MSSA coverage for skin, diabetic foot, bites, and intra-abdominal infections. — Slide 42"},{"q":"Which is the preferred macrolide therapy for urethritis due to chlamydia?","choices":["Erythromycin","Azithromycin","Clarithromycin","Clindamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Azithromycin is the preferred therapy for chlamydial urethritis. — Slide 54"},{"q":"Tetracyclines are best classified as:","choices":["Bactericidal, with concentration-dependent killing","Bacteriostatic, time-dependent with moderate PAE","Bactericidal, time-dependent with a brief PAE","Bacteriostatic, with essentially no PAE at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines are bacteriostatic, time-dependent, with moderate PAE and broad-spectrum coverage. — Slide 49"},{"q":"A distinctive adverse effect of tetracyclines (e.g., doxycycline) is:","choices":["Tendon rupture and inflammation, especially in the elderly","Tooth discoloration/deposition in bone and photosensitivity","Red man syndrome from rapid intravenous infusion","A disulfiram-like reaction when combined with alcohol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tetracyclines deposit in bone/teeth (discoloration; avoid in children) and cause photosensitivity. — Slide 51"},{"q":"Which class inhibits bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase?","choices":["Aminoglycosides","Aminopenicillins","Cephalosporins","Fluoroquinolones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase, halting DNA replication and transcription. — Slide 59"},{"q":"Tendinitis and tendon rupture are distinctive adverse effects of:","choices":["Cephalosporins","Fluoroquinolones","Sulfonamides","Aminopenicillins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fluoroquinolones carry a distinctive risk of tendinitis/tendon rupture (and cartilage concerns in children). — Slide 62"},{"q":"Which penicillin class covers Pseudomonas?","choices":["Anti-pseudomonal penicillins (piperacillin, ticarcillin)","Anti-staphylococcal penicillins (nafcillin, oxacillin)","Natural penicillins (penicillin G, penicillin V)","Extended-spectrum penicillins (amoxicillin, ampicillin)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anti-pseudomonal penicillins piperacillin and ticarcillin cover Pseudomonas. — Slide 30"},{"q":"Amoxicillin (an extended-spectrum penicillin) is specifically used prophylactically for:","choices":["Treatment of Rocky Mountain spotted fever in adults","Prevention of bacterial endocarditis in high-risk patients","Treatment of severe antibiotic-associated C. difficile colitis","Treatment of vaginal trichomoniasis infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin is used as prophylaxis to prevent bacterial endocarditis in high-risk patients. — Slide 36"},{"q":"Macrolides and clindamycin inhibit protein synthesis by targeting the:","choices":["The 30S ribosomal subunit directly directly","Cell wall penicillin-binding proteins","Bacterial DNA gyrase and topoisomerase","50S subunit (blocking translocation)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrolides and clindamycin bind the 50S subunit and inhibit translocation. — Slide 52"},{"q":"Clindamycin is distinctively associated with which adverse effect?","choices":["Tendon rupture and inflammation","Red man syndrome with rapid infusion","C. difficile / pseudomembranous colitis","Permanent tooth discoloration in children"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is strongly associated with C. difficile/pseudomembranous colitis. — Slide 56"},{"q":"Ciprofloxacin's spectrum is best described as:","choices":["Gram-positive cocci only, with no gram-negative activity","Atypical pathogens only, with no gram-negative coverage","Anaerobic organisms and intestinal parasites primarily","Gram-negative and atypical coverage, with +/- Pseudomonas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ciprofloxacin provides gram-negative and atypical coverage with variable Pseudomonas activity. — Slide 61"},{"q":"Which agent is indicated for MRSA and is bactericidal against gram-positives?","choices":["Vancomycin","Aztreonam","Cephalexin","Nalidixic acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is bactericidal against gram-positives and indicated for MRSA. — Slide 44"}]};
